.TH RANDSEED 1M "31 October 2026" "random"
.SH NAME
randseed \- keep the random generator's seed across reboots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/init.d/randseed
.B start
|
.B stop
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B randseed
runs at every boot as
.B /etc/rc2.d/S21randseed
and at every shutdown as
.BR /etc/rc0.d/K02randseed .
.TP
.B start
writes the seed saved at the last shutdown, then the output of
.B date
and
.BR "ps \-ef" ,
into
.BR /dev/urandom ,
which loads the
.B random
module, and saves a fresh seed at once, so that a crash never makes the
next boot reuse one.
.TP
.B stop
saves a fresh seed.
.PP
The seed is 512 bytes read from
.BR /dev/urandom ,
in
.BR /etc/random-seed ,
readable by root only; it replaces the old one only when it was read whole.
.PP
When the device cannot be written (a kernel without the module, or no
node), the step says so in one line on the console and in the boot log,
keeps the old seed and lets the boot go on.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/random-seed
the seed.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
the boot log:
.B randseed:
lines.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR random (7),
.BR rc.subr (1M)
